<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="323e1c10">&lt;p&gt;
    The purpose of confirming client issues is to provide a basis for planning a project. Client issues translate into
    requirements for EY’s services. The requirements and associated quality criteria may come from a variety of sources
    including:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Client business objectives
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Industry standards, laws and regulations
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Technical specifications
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Professional standards
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Stakeholder requirements
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Often clients document requirements in a request for proposal or similar document. More often client requirements must
    be uncovered in a dialogue between EY and the Client. In general, requirements should be objective statements that
    describe measurable aspects of the people, process, and technology needed to achieve the business objectives. However,
    subjective requirements such as those stated during discussions with the client or from the results of stakeholder
    analysis are also important factors to consider. Overall, the requirements should identify:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Background information (why this project, why now?)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Objectives (what is the client trying to achieve?)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Desired output
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Service delivery approach/activities
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Costs (estimated fees)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Assumptions (pre-conditions or working hypothesis)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Constraints (limitations on scope, financials, timeline, etc.)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Risks (what could go wrong?)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Quality criteria (how will we and the client measure the quality of the work products? against what criteria or
        metrics?)&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
